{"componentChunkName":"component---src-templates-blog-template-js","path":"/linux/ssh-tunnels","result":{"data":{"markdownRemark":{"html":"<h2 id=\"one-client-connects-to-a-network\" style=\"position:relative;\">One client connects to a network<a href=\"#one-client-connects-to-a-network\" aria-label=\"one client connects to a network permalink\" class=\"with-anchor after\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" height=\"20\" version=\"1.1\" viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" width=\"20\">\n                <path fill-rule=\"evenodd\" d=\"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3\n                3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3\n                  9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64\n                  1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z\">\n                </path>\n                </svg></a></h2>\n<p>We will ssh to one machine in a remote network (this doesn't make sense in a local network) and will redirect port from another machine (I used this to setup a router through a web interface).</p>\n<p>We will need two terminals. First, connect to the remote machine <strong>10.10.0.123</strong> and create a tunnel. But there is nothing on port 8888 yet.</p>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"text\"><pre class=\"language-text\"><code class=\"language-text\">ssh user@10.10.0.123 -L 8888:localhost:8888</code></pre></div>\n<p>Second, redirect to port 8888 on <strong>10.10.0.123</strong> traffic from <strong>10.10.0.1:443</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"text\"><pre class=\"language-text\"><code class=\"language-text\">ssh user@10.10.0.123 -L 8888:10.10.0.1:443</code></pre></div>\n<p>Now locally access in the browser <a href=\"https://localhost:8888\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external nofollow noopener noreferrer\">https://localhost:8888</a> which will be the same as if you are in the remote network accessing <a href=\"https://10.10.0.1:443\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external nofollow noopener noreferrer\">https://10.10.0.1:443</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"both-clients-behind-nat\" style=\"position:relative;\">Both clients behind NAT<a href=\"#both-clients-behind-nat\" aria-label=\"both clients behind nat permalink\" class=\"with-anchor after\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" height=\"20\" version=\"1.1\" viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" width=\"20\">\n                <path fill-rule=\"evenodd\" d=\"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3\n                3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3\n                  9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64\n                  1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z\">\n                </path>\n                </svg></a></h2>\n<p>They must meet at a neutral location that has a public IP address, and a ssh server listening on port 22. Let's say this address is <strong>10.20.30.40</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"client-1\" style=\"position:relative;\">Client 1<a href=\"#client-1\" aria-label=\"client 1 permalink\" class=\"with-anchor after\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" height=\"20\" version=\"1.1\" viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" width=\"20\">\n                <path fill-rule=\"evenodd\" d=\"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3\n                3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3\n                  9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64\n                  1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z\">\n                </path>\n                </svg></a></h3>\n<p>Connect to the rendezvous host and leave there an entry point available locally on port 20000</p>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"text\"><pre class=\"language-text\"><code class=\"language-text\">ssh -R 20000:127.0.0.1:22 user@10.20.30.40</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"client-2\" style=\"position:relative;\">Client 2<a href=\"#client-2\" aria-label=\"client 2 permalink\" class=\"with-anchor after\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" height=\"20\" version=\"1.1\" viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" width=\"20\">\n                <path fill-rule=\"evenodd\" d=\"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3\n                3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3\n                  9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64\n                  1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z\">\n                </path>\n                </svg></a></h3>\n<p>Run 2 commands in separate terminals. First, also connect to the rendezvous host, find there a local port 20000, and make it available locally (locally for client 2) at 21000:</p>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"text\"><pre class=\"language-text\"><code class=\"language-text\">ssh -o PubkeyAuthentication=no -L 21000:127.0.0.1:20000 user@10.20.30.40</code></pre></div>\n<p>Second, connect to the tunnel we just opened:</p>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"text\"><pre class=\"language-text\"><code class=\"language-text\">ssh -o PubkeyAuthentication=no bingo@127.0.0.1 -p 21000</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"not-ssh-solutions\" style=\"position:relative;\">Not ssh solutions<a href=\"#not-ssh-solutions\" aria-label=\"not ssh solutions permalink\" class=\"with-anchor after\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" height=\"20\" version=\"1.1\" viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" width=\"20\">\n                <path fill-rule=\"evenodd\" d=\"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3\n                3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3\n                  9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64\n                  1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z\">\n                </path>\n                </svg></a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://fowl.readthedocs.io/en/latest/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external nofollow noopener noreferrer\">fowl</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://magic-wormhole.readthedocs.io/en/latest/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external nofollow noopener noreferrer\">Magic Wormhole</a></li>\n</ul>","excerpt":"One client connects to a network We will ssh to one machine in a remote network (this doesn't make sense in a local network) and will…","tableOfContents":"<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"#one-client-connects-to-a-network\">One client connects to a network</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"#both-clients-behind-nat\">Both clients behind NAT</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"#client-1\">Client 1</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#client-2\">Client 2</a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"#not-ssh-solutions\">Not ssh solutions</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>","fields":{"socialcard":"gatsby-plugin-social-card/a2ff1a66-7a67-5946-a468-7b782c094ed7.jpg"},"frontmatter":{"date":"December 09, 2023","published":"December 09, 2023","lastModified":"December 09, 2023","title":"SSH tunnels","subtitle":null,"section":null,"draft":null,"developing":null,"buttonText":null,"buttonLink":null,"secondButtonText":null,"secondButtonLink":null,"featuredImage":null}}},"pageContext":{"showLikes":true,"absolutePath":"/home/runner/work/mikolasan.github.io/mikolasan.github.io/src/markdown/linux/ssh-tunnels.md","url":"/linux/ssh-tunnels","next":{"excerpt":"For example OpenSSL library libssl-dev Go to packages.ubuntu.com and look for download source package (link for bionic version of openssl…","fileAbsolutePath":"/home/runner/work/mikolasan.github.io/mikolasan.github.io/src/markdown/linux/how-ubuntu-package-is-compiled.md","frontmatter":{"title":"H